#---
# purpose: A closure called through a parameter the `@spec` declares as an arrow answers with the arrow's declared return, so a callsite whose only evidence is that arrow has a result type on all three paths
# root: main/0
# assert.metric.semantic.activations: 13
# assert.metric.semantic.executables: 13
# assert.metric.semantic.callsites: 9
# assert.metric.call_edges.count: 9
# oracle: self
# oracle.stamp: 608b6fb9f0fc4aeb
#---
#
# `Fmt.fmt/3` is passed as its own `enc`. Called from inside the lambda that
# `wrap` builds, `enc` is a declared arrow with no closure literal behind it,
# and the arrow's return is `iodata()`. That declared return is the call's
# answer.
#
# The spec is pinned as written: it says `fmt` returns `iodata()`, while the
# body returns a closure. Elixir does not check a spec against its body, and
# neither does this fixture; the program only asks whether the result is a
# function.
defmodule Fmt do
  @spec fmt(map(), (dynamic(), dynamic(), map() -> iodata()), map()) :: iodata()
  def fmt(m, enc, s) when is_map(m), do: wrap(m, enc, s)
  def wrap(_m, enc, s), do: fn {_k, v} -> enc.(v, enc, s) end
end

defmodule Main do
  def main() do
    IO.inspect(is_function(Fmt.fmt(%{}, &Fmt.fmt/3, %{}), 1))
  end
end

Main.main()
